cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "FusionFlux: extrapolation, long-range saturation and physics constraints in confinement scaling"
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Liam
    family-names: Salcedo
    alias: lsalcedo100
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0001-5039-8147"
repository-code: "https://github.com/lsalcedo100/FusionFlux"
abstract: >-
  Confinement scalings are fitted across existing tokamaks to size machines
  not yet built, so validation must measure performance on an unseen device.
  On the ITPA Global H-mode Confinement Database (HDB5, STD5; 6228 slices,
  18 labels), the conventional cross-validation grouped by discharge scores
  a random forest 29% better in log error than a power law refitted in the
  same folds and 36% better than published IPB98(y,2). Holding out one
  device at a time reverses the order of the three primary models: the two
  tree ensembles that win cross-validation extrapolate worst, and the forest
  loses to that power law on 13 of 13 labels. Regressing the stored energy
  instead, or in dimensionless coordinates that identify no device, leaves
  the forest worse on 9 of 13 and 11 of 13 labels. The intervals fail more
  sharply. At a cut inside the database reproducing ITER's 1.82x jump in
  major radius, nominal 90% split-conformal intervals calibrated on held-out
  discharges cover 3% and 0% of held-out rows for both ensembles at a width
  moving under 1.5%. The width cannot respond: a split-conformal half-width
  is a rank statistic of the calibration residuals, so the interval carries
  no warning that it has stopped covering. The warning does exist: the
  forest's error tracks the held-out label's Mahalanobis distance from the
  training data at rho = +0.85, against -0.06 for the power law. The fault
  is the candidate set, not cross-validation: a linear-plus-RBF Gaussian
  process, the best cross-validated model, keeps trending outside the data
  and does not reverse. At the size cut, that process and Connor-Taylor
  similarity constraints on the exponents, whose Kadomtsev rung costs
  nothing in sample, both improve extrapolation. Varying only the long-range
  mean, with the nonlinear part fixed, identifies the mechanism as
  saturation rather than flexibility. Includes a rank-deficiency audit of
  the design matrix, a from-scratch least-squares implementation (Cholesky,
  QR, SVD), a refit of the IPB98(y,2) exponents decomposed along the
  singular directions of the data, and a domain-agnostic module packaging
  the audit for other scaling-law datasets.
keywords:
  - fusion
  - tokamak
  - plasma physics
  - energy confinement time
  - scaling law
  - machine learning
  - extrapolation
  - out-of-distribution generalization
  - Gaussian processes
  - allometry
  - model selection
